abstract:
Ultrasound production of adult woodmice was measured when individual were placed in the unoccupied cage of a conspecific of the same sex. Ten-minute tests were repeated four times at 24 h intervals. The same individuals were tested 7 days later in the unoccupied cage of a conspecific of the opposite sex. Before each series of measurements, each individual met in a neutral enclosure the resident of the cage in which he was to be tested. The encounters were repeated four times at 24 h intervals. During the second series of tests the estrus was estimated indirectly.
 Under these conditions: 

 •
 in the presence of female scent, sexually active males produced ultrasounds in all tests;


 •
 their ultrasound production increased if the scent was that of an estrous female;


 •
 the ultrasonic response of females tested with male scent was more variable than that of the males; some females produced ultrasounds at a higher rate during estrus;


 •
 emission rate was lower when the mice were in the presence of scent of a conspecific of the same sex;


 •
 in some tests two types of ultrasounds could be distinguished; their proportion varied in time; one type of ultrasound was almost exclusively detected when in the unoccupied cage of a conspecific of the opposite sex; males and females produced both types of ultrasounds.
